                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Full On Paintball is a free first person paintball game that tries to turn the sport into a proper shooter rather than just using paintball as a theme. I can jump into different maps, pick my equipment and take part in several game modes while trying not to get tagged. There are speedball arenas, woodsball maps and mixed environments, so the game has a decent amount of variety on paper. I like the basic idea because paintball gives the usual shooter formula a different feel. Getting hit is supposed to matter more, and the weapons and movement are built around that rather than treating paintball guns like normal firearms.\n\nThe amount of equipment is one of the better parts for me. There are more than 20 weapons and different parts such as barrels, hoppers and grips that let me change my setup. I can also customise my character and work through ranks, skills and unlocks. That gives me something to work towards instead of every match feeling completely separate. I like having the option to build a loadout around the way I want to play, although the progression is fairly simple compared with much bigger competitive shooters.\n\nThere is also a surprising amount of content available. The game has 28 maps and 10 different game types, with dedicated servers supporting matches of up to 32 players. I like the fact that the maps are not all trying to be the same kind of arena. The difference between a compact speedball field and a larger forest map changes how I approach a match, and the bigger environments can make positioning and finding cover much more important. There is also single player support and local multiplayer features, which gives me a few options outside the main online setup.\n\nThe main problem for me is that the game feels dated. Full On Paintball was released back in 2018, and some parts of the presentation and overall feel show that age. The shooting can be fairly basic, the movement does not feel especially refined and the interface is not as clean as I would expect from a more modern shooter. I can still enjoy the idea behind it, but there are moments where I am reminded that this is a small indie project rather than a highly polished competitive FPS. The game is also very dependent on having enough people around for the multiplayer side to feel properly active, which makes the large maps and 32 player support less useful when I am playing alone.\n\nI also think the progression could have been more meaningful. Having skills, ranks and equipment upgrades sounds good, but I do not feel like they transform the way I play enough. The different weapons and customisation options give me things to experiment with, but the core loop remains pretty straightforward. I shoot, avoid getting hit, capture or defend objectives depending on the mode and repeat the process. That is perfectly fine for a simple paintball shooter, but I wanted the equipment and tactical side to have a bigger effect on the matches.\n\nWhat keeps me interested is that the game does not try to take itself too seriously. The paintball setting gives it a lighter feeling than a normal military shooter, and I enjoy the idea of running around outdoor fields with paintball markers instead of the usual collection of military weapons. The fact that the base game is free also helps a lot. There are optional paid additions for things like weapons, skins and an ATV, but I can still get into the main game without buying the extra content.\n\nOverall, Full On Paintball is a decent little shooter with a good idea behind it, but I think its age and lack of polish stop it from being something I would rate much higher. I like the large selection of maps, different game types, paintball focused equipment and simple progression, and I appreciate that the main game is free. The shooting and presentation are fairly rough, and the multiplayer side can be limited by how active the game is, which makes some of the advertised scale less meaningful to me. Still, when I want a simple paintball themed FPS without spending anything, there is enough here for me to have some fun with it.\n\nRating: 6.9/10\n",
